                "text": "I'm figuring out the rule connecting vertical and horizontal lines through gaps in the grid: the horizontal line's gap column matches where the vertical line sits, and each line's color fills the space on its own side of the intersection. Looking at the training example, I'm tracing how the gap at column 3 aligns with the vertical line's column to determine the output coloring.\n\nNow applying this to the test case, I'm working through multiple horizontal lines that share the same gap column (col1), so I need to figure out how the vertical line at col1 segments into different colors between each horizontal row - color above the first gap, a different color between the two horizontals, and the vertical's own color below the last one. I'm doing the same analysis for column 9, checking whether the gap positions there actually align consistently across rows.\n\nChecking the actual training example confirms gaps just get filled in place, not extended like a solid color takes over \u2014 so row3 becomes fully 6, and the vertical gaps at col1/col9 get filled with the color of whichever horizontal line is nearest below them. Now I'm mapping out each row of the grid with these fills applied, row by row.",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
